course: dictionary
lesson: 10858
title: Words that shake
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To vibrate means to move quickly and gently backwards and forwards, or from side to side, many times every second. It is a small, fast shake. You can often see it, feel it, or hear the result.
explain: Context gives you the clues. «A short, tight guitar string vibrates fast and sounds high; a long, loose string vibrates slowly and sounds low.» The words fast and high are next to vibrates, so vibrating must be about how quickly something shakes. A slower shake gives a lower sound.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings and makes them vibrate.» If you put shake quickly back to and fro in place of vibrate, the sentence still makes sense, so you have got it.
fact: To vibrate means to move quickly and gently backwards and forwards, or from side to side, many times a second.
fact: Vibrate is a verb: vibrate, vibrates, vibrated, vibrating.
fact: Sound is made when something vibrates, like a guitar string, a drum skin or our vocal cords.
fact: Faster vibrations make a higher sound, and slower vibrations make a lower sound.
fact: Phones vibrate when a message comes, so you can feel it without the phone ringing.
exercise: «A short, tight guitar string vibrates fast and sounds high.» What does vibrates mean here? | Stays perfectly still | *Moves quickly backwards and forwards many times a second | Changes colour | Gets longer and looser
exercise: «The sitar has sympathetic strings that nobody plucks; they vibrate by themselves when matching notes are played.» What makes these strings vibrate? | *Matching notes being played | Their own weight | Somebody plucking them | The wind blowing through the room
exercise: Mum says, «Put your phone on vibrate in the cinema.» What should the phone do when a message comes? | Turn into a torch | Switch off for ever | *Shake gently so you feel it | Ring loudly for everyone to hear
test: «Rosin is hardened pine resin rubbed on a bow so the hair grips the strings and makes them vibrate.» What do the strings do when the bow grips them? | *Vibrate | Fall silent for ever | Melt | Turn into horsehair
test: «In pan-pipes the shorter pipes play higher notes, because a shorter column of air vibrates faster.» Which air vibrates faster? | *The shorter column of air | Both exactly the same | Neither, because nothing vibrates | The longer column of air
exam: Faster vibrations make which kind of sound? | Longer | *Higher | Lower | Quieter
